@HostAddress = http://localhost:5166

### ================= Setup =================

### Create a category to attach every subscription below to
# @name createCategory
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Categories
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Renewals-{{$guid}}"
}

### ================= Case 1: start date in the future =================
### The first renewal IS the start date itself, even though it hasn't happened yet.

# @name futureStart
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "FutureStart-{{$guid}}",
  "description": "Starts in the future",
  "price": 9.99,
  "billingCycle": 1,
  "startDate": "2027-06-15T00:00:00Z",
  "categoryId": {{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
}

### Expect it in the list with nextRenewalDate = 2027-06-15 (well beyond the default window, so use a wide one)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/upcoming?withinDays=100000
Accept: application/json

### ================= Case 2: Day billing cycle, started long ago =================
### Every day is an occurrence, so the next one is always today.

# @name dailyStartedLongAgo
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "DailyLongAgo-{{$guid}}",
  "description": "Billed every day",
  "price": 0.5,
  "billingCycle": 0,
  "startDate": "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "categoryId": {{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
}

### Expect it in the list with nextRenewalDate = today
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/upcoming
Accept: application/json

### ================= Case 3: Monthly cycle starting on day 31 (the fixed clipping bug) =================
### Before the fix, stepping Jan 31 -> Feb 28 -> Mar 28 -> Apr 28 (compounding the clip from
### the short February) instead of correctly landing back on day 31/30 every other month.
### After the fix, every occurrence is computed fresh from Jan 31, so short months only clip
### that one occurrence (e.g. Feb 28) without dragging every later month down with it.

# @name monthlyDay31
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "MonthlyDay31-{{$guid}}",
  "description": "Started on the 31st",
  "price": 9.99,
  "billingCycle": 1,
  "startDate": "2026-01-31T00:00:00Z",
  "categoryId": {{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
}

### Inspect nextRenewalDate here: it must be the correct last day of the upcoming month
### (e.g. the 30th or 31st, never stuck on 28 unless the upcoming month really is February)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/upcoming?withinDays=60
Accept: application/json

### ================= Case 4: Yearly cycle starting on Feb 29 (leap day) =================
### Same clipping concern as Case 3, but year-by-year: in a non-leap year it lands on Feb 28
### for that one year only, not for every year after it.

# @name yearlyLeapDay
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "YearlyLeapDay-{{$guid}}",
  "description": "Started on Feb 29",
  "price": 49.99,
  "billingCycle": 2,
  "startDate": "2024-02-29T00:00:00Z",
  "categoryId": {{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
}

### Inspect nextRenewalDate here: Feb 28 in a non-leap year, Feb 29 in a leap year - never
### drifting to a different month
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/upcoming?withinDays=600
Accept: application/json

### ================= Case 5: paused subscriptions are excluded =================

# @name toBePaused
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "ToBePaused-{{$guid}}",
  "description": "Will be paused",
  "price": 9.99,
  "billingCycle": 1,
  "startDate": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "categoryId": {{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
}

### Pause it
PUT {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/{{toBePaused.response.body.$.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "newStatus": 1
}

### Expect it NOT in the list, even with a huge window
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/upcoming?withinDays=100000
Accept: application/json

### ================= Case 6: EndDate before the computed next renewal =================

# @name toBeEnded
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "ToBeEnded-{{$guid}}",
  "description": "Will end before its next renewal",
  "price": 9.99,
  "billingCycle": 1,
  "startDate": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "categoryId": {{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
}

### Set an EndDate in the past
PUT {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/{{toBeEnded.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "ToBeEnded",
  "description": "Will end before its next renewal",
  "billingCycle": 1,
  "startDate": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "endDate": "2026-01-02T00:00:00Z",
  "categoryId": {{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
}

### Expect it NOT in the list, even with a huge window
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/upcoming?withinDays=100000
Accept: application/json

### ================= Case 7: withinDays boundary =================
### A fixed near-future date so a narrow window excludes it and a wide one includes it.
### If you're running this file long after it was written, bump the date further out so it's
### still in the future relative to "today".

# @name nearFutureStart
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "NearFutureStart-{{$guid}}",
  "description": "Starts a couple of weeks out",
  "price": 9.99,
  "billingCycle": 1,
  "startDate": "2026-12-01T00:00:00Z",
  "categoryId": {{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
}

### A narrow window excludes it...
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/upcoming?withinDays=1
Accept: application/json

### ...a wide one includes it
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/Subscriptions/upcoming?withinDays=100000
Accept: application/json

### ================= Note =================
### DbUpdateExceptionHandler's distinction between a real constraint violation (409, already
### exercised above and in the main .http file) and a transient SQLite error (left as an
### unhandled 500) can't be triggered through a single manual request - it needs genuine
### concurrent writes. That case is covered by DbUpdateExceptionHandlerTests instead.

### Trying to delete the category created above still returns 409: none of the subscriptions
### created in this file were deleted, so it still has subscriptions assigned to it.
DELETE {{HostAddress}}/api/Categories/{{createCategory.response.body.$.id}}
